 = b_{1}u$ is a solution of the congruence $a_{1}x≡b_{1}(modn_{1})$.\n> $L10$: Now, it is true that $a_{1}x_{i}≡b_{1}(modn_{1})$, for $x_{i} = x_{1} + (i - 1)n_{1}, 1 \\le i \\le k$.\n> $L11$: Therefore, $(ka_{1})x_{i}≡(kb_{1})(modkn_{1})$.\n> $L12$: Thus, $ax_{i}≡b(modn)$.\n> $L13$: This means that $x_{i}$ is solution for original congruence $ax≡b(modn)$ for each $1 \\le i \\le k$.\n> $L14$: Now, suppose that $i, j ∈ {1, …, k - 1}, i \\ne j$ and $x_{i}≡x_{j}(modn)$.\n> $L15$: Then, $x_{1} + (i - 1)n_{1}≡x_{1} + (j - 1)n_{1}(modn)$.\n> $L16$: So, $(i - 1)n_{1}≡(j - 1)n_{1}(modn_{1}k)$.\n> $L17$: And we get that $i≡j(modk)$.\n> $L18$: Therefore, $i = j$, which is a contradiction.\n> $L19$: This means that the solutions $x_{i}$ are distinct and mutually non-congruent modulo $n$.\n> $L20$: Finally, we conclude that the congruence has exactly $k$ distinct solutions modulo $n$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from {{field-1}} in line $L1$ by {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L8$ follows from line {{field-3}} by the fact that {{field-4}}.\n\nLine $L17$ follows from line $L16$ by the {{field-5}}."}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Number of Solutions to a Congruence" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050077 :topic/math-academy-id 4941 :topic/title "Proving Properties of Linear Congruences"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203956 :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "reflexive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200821} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200822} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "irreflexive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200823}] :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200820} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} 